SM(561,{"n":"2 Corinthians 4 Conclusion","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/2 Corinthians 4 Conclusion.mp3","d":2732.5,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.96,"Verse 5, for we do not preach ourselves, but Christ Jesus the Lord, and ourselves your bondservants for Jesus' sake."],[32.69,"He had just talked about how he lived his life."],[35.27,"But he's making it clear."],[36.39,"Look, this isn't about me."],[38.6,"This is about me not getting in the way of Christ, who's the one I'm actually preaching."],[43.74,"Because bondservant is a nice way of saying slave."],[47.92,"So St. Paul says, In Christ, I'm your slave."],[52.72,"He doesn't just say he's Christ's slave."],[54.16,"'s slave. He says he their"],[55.84,"Right? So he's out of the way."],[64.41,"He's just their serving, trying to connect them to Christ."],[73.37,"For it is the God who commanded light to shine out of darkness, who has shown in our hearts to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ."],[85.77,"So the same God who, Genesis 1, said, Let there be light."],[93.51,"He gave the command."],[94.92,"And there was light that showed out of the darkness."],[98.52,"It's that same God who has shown his light into our hearts."],[103.14,"So that we could see Christ."],[104.82,"That light of Christ's glory."],[107.54,"Okay. Now notice this last part, a note on this last part."],[116.9,"The light of the knowledge of the glory of God, which is what he was just talking about."],[121.54,"And then he says, We see that in the face of Jesus Christ."],[128.5,"Now the apostles, of course, lived with Christ."],[131.56,"Plenty of people in Galilee and Judea saw Jesus at various points."],[135.26,"Because he was preaching publicly, had these big crowds."],[138.34,"Saint Paul, as we saw in the book of Acts, had seen Christ personally at least three times."],[143.44,"Christ had appeared to be."],[145.56,"When had anyone in Corinth seen the face of Jesus Christ?"],[151.56,"Well, not in the literal physical sense."],[156.84,"That was as easy as so allow me to submit to you the idea of the antiquity of iconography."],[176.93,"Because what has Saint Paul just been talking about?"],[181.81,"He's been talking about prayer, focus."],[185.91,"The focusing of the mind on Christ."],[188.25,"That's what he's been talking about."],[190.81,"So let me tell you about where Corinthians prayed."],[197.5,"The place where they didn't they didn't sort of go to the temple every day to make sacrifices."],[202.14,"There are big feasts where there were temple sacrifices."],[204.62,"They didn't have one day a week."],[206.64,"The way the Jews did, and the way we do when pagans went, you're right."],[210.4,"Like they didn't have Friday, Saturday, or Sunday where they all went to temple."],[214.16,"Pagan Corinthians, yeah."],[215.84,"Which they all, all these people who he's writing to, or at least the vast majority were."],[222.57,"Before before they became Christians."],[225.14,"Uh so that's not how they primarily practice their religion, at least in terms of prayer."],[229.82,"prayer. The place they practiced was at their hearth"],[235.3,"In their house, in their home, in the family home."],[238.58,"They had a hearth."],[240.02,"And as pagans, on that hearth, they had the family gods."],[245.48,"They had little figures and idols and images."],[249.58,"Of the gods that belong to the family."],[251.5,"Because you sort of inherited your gods in the ancient world."],[255.74,"We have this idea of pantheons, because we read mythology."],[259.68,"They didn't have mythology."],[261.56,"They had religious practices."],[263.57,"And so the gods that you worshiped were primarily a function of number one, your family, because your family would have gods who your ancestors had served for centuries."],[276.39,"Uh and uh where you were from and where you lived."],[281.69,"The gods of the place where you were born, and then if it was different, the gods of whatever place you moved to."],[287.77,"And those would all kind of get sort of piled together."],[291.58,"Sort of. Um when you say heart, I know the fireplace wasn't in the middle till the middle ages."],[298.71,"This is the place where they did the cooking."],[300.87,"Okay, okay. This is the place where they did the cooking, and so the place vital place."],[306.32,"Yeah. Yeah, central."],[307.7,"I mean, Vesta, the the Roman god is the goddess of the the hearth."],[312.4,"Um, and yeah, so this was the center of family life."],[314.24,"Okay And that's where these that's where these things would be."],[321.29,"Um so when someone becomes a Christian, obviously, they're not gonna have those around anymore."],[328.11,"The idols and all of that."],[329.79,"But they're still going to pray."],[333.63,"They're still gonna have a place where they go."],[337.47,"Okay. And if you look at we have some perfectly preserved Roman villas because of Pompeii."],[347.55,"Um because of Mount Vesuvius erupting."],[350.19,"Um and all of their walls were covered with imagery from what we would call mythology."],[357.47,"Some of it very sexually graphic."],[359.99,"Because that's how Greek and Roman mythology was."],[362.71,"But all of these things all over the walls."],[366.67,"Those likewise, if they're Christians would have been caught."],[371.15,"We've never gone into a house, including a house that was used as a church, and found whitewashed walls in Christian houses."],[378.83,"Meaning they had Christian iconography."],[385.07,"So I would submit to you that the only logical understanding within this context, talking about prayer."],[393.01,"And focus and connection to Christ, where they would see the face of Jesus Christ, would be if they had iconography of Christ at their family hearth where they went and prayed."],[408.01,"I'm open to someone else giving me another contextual explanation."],[417.42,"So, you know, a lot of a lot of commentators will look at this and just say, well, this is just talking about the incarnation."],[423.12,"The glory of God is in the face of Jesus Christ."],[424.64,"Well, yeah, that's true."],[425.84,"That's true. But are you saying to me that St."],[429.44,"Paul, writing this letter to Corinth that's experiencing all these problems."],[434.6,"Just decided to drop in a nugget about the incarnation?"],[439.44,"Like in the middle of this discussion of prayer and focus and looking upon the glory of of Chr Christist. and living your life out of that in the community and shining the light to the community"],[452.02,"He's just like, oh yeah, by the way, the incarnation."],[457.71,"In terms of the instructions he's giving them, I don't see another way in which a reference to the face of Christ makes a whole lot of immediate sense to your average just Corinthian Christian."],[473.79,"So I think that's important enough to pause on."],[476.85,"And to and to note that this seems very much to be a reference to iconography and to connect it with prayer."],[485.17,"This is in the 50s AD."],[491.15,"And makes sense in terms of how they as ancient people understood prayer and these things."],[501.39,"That's good. So you can you can annoy your Calvinist friends with that."],[510.03,"Oh, okay. Well, you've got a sister who's nominally, nominally at least."],[516.99,"Yeah. Okay. So verse 7, St."],[524.03,"Paul has been talking about, remember, he's talking about this glory."],[527.33,"The glory of God, the glory of Christ, and making it manifest, trying to make it manifest through him himsself.elf, not by being glorious, but by getting out of the way"],[538.97,"And allowing this light of Christ to shine."],[541.45,"Okay, so he says, verse 7, but we have this treasure."],[545.31,"The glory of God in Christ."],[547.95,"We have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us."],[557.39,"Paul is making an analogy here."],[561.11,"That uh this light of the glory is contained in an earthen vessel."],[570.03,"Here, we'll ask the I'll ask the uh the special bonus question."],[574.59,"If you want all the brownie points."],[576.71,"Could tell me what old testament story Saint Paul is obliquely referencing."],[583.71,"This is obscure."],[585.39,"That's why you'll get all the brownie points."],[587.47,"Okay, I won't leave you hanging."],[588.75,"There's something in Jeremiah, I think."],[590.51,"It's in Judges, actually."],[592.11,"Everyone's favorite book of sex and violence."],[595.47,"Uh Gideon. When they're going to attack the Midianites, they put earthenware pots over their torches so that they won't see the light as they approach."],[609.15,"Oh. Oh, I'd have gotten that sh-."],[614.35,"Yeah. That's why I didn't leave you hanging."],[617.15,"So remember, say St."],[620.75,"Paul's picking up the language he's been using, which has been what?"],[622.75,"Gospel is the report of a victory"],[625.99,"Remember he used that example of a Roman triumph, this triumphal procession?"],[630.23,"Yeah. The light of the glory of God, it's like this torch, but there's this earthenware pot over it."],[636.53,"Uh-huh. There's this earthenware pot over it."],[639.59,"Uh and who's the earthenware pot in this analogy?"],[642.71,"The earthenware pot is Saint Paul."],[646.01,"And we may think earthenware pot, that may sound kind of nice to us."],[649.21,"We go to these nifty little craft crafty shops and stuff and get pottery."],[653.55,"For decorative purposes."],[655.07,"Okay. Earthenware pots were the trash of the ancient world."],[662.35,"There's broken pieces of pottery everywhere in the ancient world and from them we know that they used it like for scratch paper right they didn't have paper you know we have little torn off bits of scratch paper with notes written on them and stuff post-its and that kind of thing they used old pottery shards because they were laying everywhere you'd just pick one up and scratch something and scratch a note into it right or whatever when they took when they took votes in early Greek democracy uh they used they used pottery shards they put it on a pottery shard scratch it in and and and turn them in it was like scratch paper um in fact, linguistic tidbit, the word for one of those pottery shards in Greek is ostraca."],[705.63,"And uh that's actually where we get the word ostracize in English, because uh they would vote people out of cities, really like Athens in the period of democracy."],[718.19,"If there was somebody who was just a troublemaker, they would vote to exile them, and they'd take the vote on those pot shirts, and so that's why having that vote go against you became known as being ostracized, and we get the English word ostracized to sort of expel someone or shun someone from that."],[736.83,"But anyway, uh so earth just pottery sharp ceramic pottery."],[740.83,"This guy's this is the trash of the ancient world."],[743.71,"This is like paper bags."],[745.4,"Or plastic bags from the supermarket today."],[748.3,"So that's what you have to think."],[749.66,"So St. Paul's saying, like, here is the light of the glory of God and the face of Jesus Christ."],[754.54,"of I'm sort like a plastic bag that got thrown over."],[760.03,"That's what he's comparing himself to."],[762.27,"But he says, why?"],[763.65,"Why would God do that?"],[764.69,"Why would God send a schmuck like me, to go and bring this, try and shine this light, bring this powerful message."],[771.92,"And he says, so it would be obvious that the whole power behind it, when people heard it and they they felt the power of God in it, and it started transforming people's lives."],[782.62,"They know, well, it's clearly not from this old paper bag."],[787.39,"It's clearly not."],[788.99,"This clearly isn't something that that this uh St."],[791.47,"Paul guy came up with on his own."],[794.29,"This is the power of God."],[801.15,"Verse eight. We are hard pressed on every side yet not crushed."],[806.43,"We are perplexed but not in despair, persecuted but not forsaken, struck down but not destroyed, always carrying about in the body the dying of the Lord Jesus, that the life of Jesus may also be manifested in our body."],[821.55,"So he's talking about, he's going back to again all these things he's been suffering."],[826.56,"But this isn't just him, he's also talking to the Corinthians so they can understand."],[830.44,"Because the Corinthian community and everyone in it has been through all this strife now."],[836.22,"And uh, you know, contrary to what certain preachers would say today, you know, hey, become a Christian and have your best life now."],[844.28,"Everything's gonna be great once you're a Christian, your life's gonna go great."],[848.04,"Our community's beautiful."],[849.56,"I didn't say his name."],[853.86,"Um that's not what they're experiencing, we'll put it that way."],[857.94,"Right in Corinth."],[859.48,"And St. Paul is saying, yeah, no doubt."],[861.89,"St. Paul's getting stoned and beat with rods and run from town to town, and you know, he's going through the whole thing."],[868.98,"But because, even though he's the old limp plastic bag, because he's got this glory of God shining in his heart, even though he's been hard pressed. on every side, he hasn't been crushed"],[883.38,"Even though he's been perplexed, the perplexed is a nice translation, vexed."],[888.28,"He doesn't fall into despair."],[890.21,"He's been persecuted, but he hasn't been forsaken by God."],[893.01,"He's been struck down, but he hasn't been destroyed."],[895.98,"He's always carrying around with him, as he said, the the dying of the Lord Jesus."],[900.72,"Christ's crucifixion. You look at him, and that's what you see."],[904.66,"The Corinthians look around them in the church with what they've been going through, and that's what they see is a lot of suffering."],[911.16,"And a lot of a lot of shame and a lot of humiliation and embarrassment."],[916.28,"Remember, people were crucified naked."],[919.09,"This was a way of humiliation in addition to torture. and death"],[924.09,"That's what they've been experiencing."],[926.15,"But the promise is that, so that."],[929.53,"In order that the life of Jesus also may be manifested in our body."],[933.55,"Meaning the resurrection."],[936.8,"St. Paul says, we're going through this suffering now."],[941.81,"So that will also participate in Christ's resurrection."],[948.31,"His life, his eternal life."],[950.17,"Because Christ is never going to die again."],[952.57,"He said that somewhere else."],[954.33,"The suffering of God hasn't been does not equate to the world to God."],[962.69,"Verse 11 for we who live are always delivered to death for Jesus' sake, that the life of Jesus also may be manifested in our mortal flesh."],[973.01,"So then death is working in us, but life in you."],[977.86,"So he sort of repeats what he just said."],[983.94,"And notice it's that we're going to experience Christ's life, the resurrection in our mortal body."],[989.16,"By going back to 1 Corinthians 15."],[992.37,"This isn't just some mental thing."],[995.63,"Some afterlife. This is the resurrection and restoration of our bodies too, our whole selves."],[1002.31,"Our whole selves suffer now."],[1004.83,"Our whole selves will experience resurrection and healing and healing in Christ."],[1010.33,"And he says this is going back to that theme that he's their slave."],[1014.78,"He's saying, so I'm manifesting, you look at me and you can see the death."],[1020.56,"But that's so that he's like, I'm going through this, I'm experiencing this for your sake to bring that light to you so that you can then experience the resurrection."],[1031.36,"Is he talking about the resurrection of the dead, or is he talking about something in this present body?"],[1038.24,"But he's talking about it."],[1039.68,"Yes. Okay. Yeah, yeah."],[1045.42,"We talk about the life of the world to come, like in the creed."],[1049.67,"But that's not something that starts in the world to come."],[1055.77,"Eternal life, uh, and and this is very clear in St."],[1059.69,"John's gospel. Eternal life starts now."],[1062.42,"That's why Christ says he who believes, lives, and believes in me will never die."],[1069.04,"He doesn't mean you're not going to physically die, but he means eternal life starts now and goes on forever."],[1076.58,"Into the age to come."],[1079.03,"So the the uh this is what's important about that, because St."],[1088.63,"Paul, by, by talking to them about this talking to them about prayer and this ministry."],[1093.41,"The things that we're doing, he's gonna get more into this as we go through the epistle, but the things that we do in this life related to the ministry of Jesus Christ, the work of God in this world, survive into the age to come."],[1108.3,"We've got the devil who's the God of this age, of this world."],[1112.48,"And all those things we were just talking about, the people are worshiping and serving that lead to them ultimately following him and that lead to destruction."],[1120.1,"Wealth, beauty, all those things."],[1121.99,"They die with it, they end when this age ends."],[1125.03,"They end when this world ends."],[1127.21,"But the things that Saint Paul is teaching them them, if they put into their life now, those don't"],[1136.79,"Those are already things that belong to the age to come."],[1142.07,"Christ says it a different way when he talks about treasures in heaven versus treasures on earth."],[1147.69,"But both of those are the result of what we do here."],[1151.13,"I can work here to lay up treasures on earth."],[1154.91,"In a warehouse."],[1155.89,"Or in my bank account."],[1157.02,"Or I can work here to lay up treasures in heaven."],[1160.14,"And then when this world ends, the ones I laid up on this earth, or when my life in this world ends, the things I laid up on this earth go poof."],[1167.92,"But the things I've laid up in heaven in this life are still there in the age to come."],[1173.64,"So does that mean since most of us are a mixture of the mixture at best."],[1182.2,"Yeah. Yeah. Does that mean that when we die, those bad parts of us are gone."],[1193.88,"The good parts of the gone."],[1195.08,"He's gonna talk about that."],[1196.36,"Oh, okay. About how they get burned up."],[1198.92,"But uh, we will get there."],[1202.92,"But hey, at least you're on track with where he's going."],[1206.76,"So yes, that is where St."],[1209.08,"Paul is going."],[1214.28,"And since we have the same spirit of faith in verse 13, according to what is written, I believed and therefore I spoke."],[1223.6,"We also believe and therefore speak."],[1226.72,"Knowing that he who raised up the Lord Jesus will also raise us up with Jesus and will present us with you."],[1236.8,"So um he says, we have uh the same uh spirit of faithfulness."],[1248.16,"We talked about how faith."],[1249.62,"See, it gets disguised here in English."],[1251.7,"Word faith and the word believe that we see in these verses is the same word."],[1255.94,"It's forms of the same word."],[1259.11,"Um but it's it's uh they're different concepts in English."],[1265.29,"That's so it's easy, it's easy for that to get lost, um to get lost in the English."],[1273.05,"Um we have the same spirit of faithfulness."],[1279.26,"And as I've also said many times and is applied, should be applied here, the word spirit, when it's used in the New Testament, should almost always be capitalized."],[1292.3,"Okay. This isn't just talking about a general spirit of faithfulness."],[1299.21,"It's talking about the spirit, especially if it's got a definite article in front of it, it"],[1303.09,"It ought to be capitalized."],[1303.93,"ed. Remember, he's talking about the spirit as oppos to the letter"],[1304.55,"We have these, and he's just been talking about the whole last chapter."],[1311.09,"We have the spirit, of faithfulness."],[1313.9,"And that word of, um, when when people are translating Greek into English, and you see them do this, the blank of blank, they're punting."],[1326.7,"What I mean by that is uh this is a this is a type of noun called a genitive, and it could be related to the other noun in a whole bunch of different ways."],[1337.34,"And by just putting of, they have decided not to figure it out."],[1343.0,"So the spirit of faithfulness, if it's out of context, could mean the faithful spirit."],[1351.06,"It could mean the spirit which causes us to be faithful."],[1356.04,"It could be right."],[1356.92,"There's any number of different ways you could read it."],[1359.88,"But just by putting just putting spirit of faithfulness, you just punt."],[1363.62,"That's something. So but in context here."],[1370.2,"We have the spirit of faith or faithfulness."],[1373.8,"According to what is written, I believed and therefore I spoke."],[1377.8,"That's a quotation from Sunset."],[1379.98,"Yeah, and I don't have Orthodox Study Bible typo."],[1387.1,"Uh it should be in the footnote."],[1388.86,"a It does not have footnote, it does not have an actual A footnote."],[1396.5,"But um they don't have a note for verse 13, can they?"],[1406.74,"So um, but so we have that the spirit dwells within us."],[1412.55,"The spirit lies behind what is written."],[1416.65,"And said, I believed."],[1418.5,"We can translate that to be consistent."],[1420.6,"Translate things the same way in the same verse, the same sentence."],[1424.76,"Uh that that, I was I was faithful and therefore I spoke."],[1428.78,"Okay that makes more sense."],[1429.02,"and therefore I spoke. I was faithful"],[1437.12,"We also are faithful and therefore speak."],[1443.49,"Based on what we just read."],[1446.7,"Based on what we just read."],[1448.78,"What is the speaking that St."],[1450.62,"Paul is doing?"],[1451.98,"The speaking is showing the light of the glory of God."],[1457.48,"Without a veil, without a covering, without any of these things, with integrity of his life."],[1464.02,"So because St."],[1465.14,"Paul is faithful to the calling he's received, even though he's an old bag."],[1471.51,"He's faithful to the calling he's received, and so he speaks."],[1477.71,"And so he teaches."],[1479.39,"Even though, as he's just said, he's not worthy of the message he's bringing."],[1484.8,"The only reason someone like him is bringing it is God wanted to make it obvious that it was coming directly from him."],[1491.21,"Sort of like Balaam's donkey."],[1493.67,"The donkey wasn't coming up with that."],[1495.67,"If a donkey's talking to you, it must be coming from God."],[1500.21,"Because donkeys can't come up with that on their own."],[1502.74,"So, so. And so, but because he's faithful to the calling, he's obedient."],[1509.47,"He speaks. He speaks."],[1512.45,"That's why he's diligent to do this."],[1512.85,"he. And so, of course,'s urging them to do the same"],[1520.47,"You're not worthy of it."],[1523.05,"You're not worthy to bring the message you're calling to bring."],[1525.77,"None of us is, but you need to be faithful and do it and shine this light."],[1531.63,"For the glory of God."],[1533.87,"Okay. And so he does that because he knows."],[1540.99,"Again, knowing, anytime you see a ger, an English gerund in a translation into English from Greek, they're punting."],[1547.8,"Because that's a participle."],[1549.56,"They didn't know how to you get you get marked down in Greek classes in graduate school if you do this."],[1558.56,"Because they know you're punting."],[1560.26,"You get this participle, it could mean because I know, it could be while I know, it could be when I know."],[1568.04,"But you just put knowing because that's nice and vague, and you punt."],[1572.2,"So they did that here too."],[1573.4,"It's actually causal."],[1574.6,"It's because we know."],[1577.88,"So actually, no, a committee translated this, and they probably all disagreed on how it should be, and so they just went with the ambiguous."],[1591.22,"So it's bec because we know."],[1594.71,"Because, or in this case, because I know, St."],[1596.79,"Paul's speaking in the first person, because I know that. he who raised up the Lord Jesus will also raise us up with Jesus and will present us with you"],[1606.11,"So the reason why Saint Paul is faithful to do this, even though he's not worthy of it, even though he's facing all this adversity, all these things are happening to him, the reason he presses on."],[1619.57,"He speaks because he's going to be faithful to his calling, and because he knows that that uh the same God, that that God the Father, who raised up, raised up Jesus Christ, is also going to raise him up."],[1635.8,"The resurrection he was just talking about, and present, present along with you."],[1640.94,"you. This is true of too"],[1641.95,"So St. Paul again is bringing it back to them."],[1645.71,"This is why I do what I do, but this is also true for you, and why you need to do this."],[1651.55,"Not to be like me because I'm so great."],[1654.35,"I'm the old pot, but because as I'm trying to be faithful, you need to be faithful."],[1665.23,"For all things are for your sakes, that grace, having spread through the many, may cause thanksgiving to abound to the glory of God."],[1676.39,"So he says, all these things I'm doing."],[1681.13,"All these things I'm doing, I'm doing for your sake, not just Corinth, but all the people to whom he's bringing the gospel, all these churches."],[1690.47,"All these things he's doing, all these things he's going through, are for their sakes."],[1695.29,"And then he talks about what that actually means."],[1697.77,"Because what has he just said he's doing?"],[1700.81,"He's doing not much."],[1702.09,"He's being a bag."],[1704.01,"He's being a pot."],[1705.37,"He's, if anything, trying not to get in the way."],[1709.9,"That means what God is doing."],[1712.78,"And that's why he talks about grace."],[1715.02,"Because grace is God acting."],[1718.3,"Grace is God acting in his creation."],[1723.19,"For all things are for your sakes, that grace having spread through the many."],[1726.87,"So it's actually God acting through Saint Paul"],[1733.13,"And when they shine the light of the glory of God in their community, that's God acting through them."],[1738.49,"Through men. And so as God's grace goes out, through his people into the world, what does that produce?"],[1748.45,"It causes thanksgiving to abound to the glory of God."],[1753.43,"From those who have received that grace and that healing."],[1756.56,"And that that mercy from God."],[1761.6,"This is something that keeps striking me."],[1764.4,"If I read that verse 15, I would think, oh my own, about hearing anything you said, I would think this is a bunch of platitudes, this is church talk, and I don't know what it means."],[1779.24,"Or it doesn't even mean anything."],[1781.88,"So it's like a door."],[1786.04,"Yeah. It's frustrating that you know millions of people are reading verse 15 and not getting the point."],[1800.12,"Yeah. Yeah. Well, this is a problem with some of our friends who want to say, well, people could just read the Bible and understand it."],[1811.4,"Yeah. I mean, we saw in the in when we're going through Acts."],[1815.48,"The Ethiopian eunuch saying they're reading Isaiah, and uh Saint Philip comes up and says, Do you understand what you're reading?"],[1824.28,"And he says, What?"],[1825.0,"How can I understand unless someone teaches me?"],[1830.3,"There's there's never a promise in the Bible that the Bible is gonna function by itself."],[1839.82,"And that makes sense because God's never done that."],[1843.5,"He's never dropped the Bible into a village somewhere that's never heard the gospel."],[1853.32,"He sends people there with the Bible."],[1856.28,"To translate the Bible into their language."],[1861.16,"To teach them about it."],[1862.68,"To explain it to them, to preach it to them."],[1865.98,"To lead them in worship where scripture is read in this context."],[1872.19,"That's how the Bible functions in the Bible."],[1875.33,"And that's how it's functioned all through the history of Christ's church."],[1880.45,"It's never just sort of sat there independently."],[1884.43,"All by itself."],[1885.88,"And so that's first response to that."],[1889.1,"God never promised that someone would be able to pick up 2 Corinthians 4 15 and read it and, oh."],[1894.26,"And oh I see."],[1897.58,"Okay. In fact, in fact, if you want to talk about what the Bible says about this, uh, Saint Peter, one of his epistles, says that there are many things in Saint in Paul's epistles that are hard to understand."],[1911.96,"Well, if they're hard for Saint Peter to understand, I'm in real trouble."],[1918.41,"So, yeah, so the the Bible even says it's hard to understand what St."],[1923.69,"Paul is saying a lot of the time."],[1927.61,"Um, but the other thing is there there is an obstacle that when you when you talked about church words, that is an obstacle for us as modern people."],[1938.11,"Because we say a lot of things and we don't know what they mean."],[1945.82,"This is something I realized very early on as a priest."],[1949.66,"The first time I tried to do catechism with somebody who was coming into the Orthodox Church."],[1956.22,"And I very quickly realized that when I said prayer and when they said prayer, we didn't mean the same thing."],[1961.98,"And to a certain extent, when I said God and they said God, we didn't mean the same thing."],[1968.08,"And that and that we were using words that, you know, maybe we didn't have any definition at all."],[1973.28,"At least we definitely didn't have one in common, like grace."],[1977.68,"Or mercy. Or all of these, all of these words, you know, and whole phrases in Christianese. have come into existence, you know, having a personal relationship with Jesus"],[1990.34,"What does that mean?"],[1992.98,"We say it all the time."],[1994.34,"Yeah. I mean, what does that mean?"],[1996.18,"Having a personal relationship, you mean like you go places with him and like no, I mean really, that's what we usually mean by personal relationship."],[2004.9,"I mean, if I say I have a personal relationship with another human, that's what I mean."],[2009.06,"We go and do stuff together, we talk on the phone."],[2011.88,"Obviously, you don't mean that about Christ."],[2015.09,"Obviously, that's not literally what you mean."],[2017.57,"So and most of the people who I've pressed on that."],[2023.67,"And I probably said it at some point in the past."],[2026.25,"And I when I said it, I didn't know what I meant."],[2029.23,"That's you know, it we just sort of say these things all the time."],[2036.59,"And so they become this kind of Christianese."],[2039.23,"And so then, especially when we translate scripture or prayers or hymns are written in Christianese, and just use those phrases."],[2048.21,"Now we've got whole texts that we have no clue what they mean because it's just jargon."],[2054.7,"Strung together, strung together in order."],[2057.1,"So, yeah, I've you know what there there are a number of tricks in this Bible study, and we'll leave this in the recording for podcast listeners, that make me seem smarter than I am."],[2069.82,"And one of those tricks is just the fact that I've spent like three decades studying Greek and so I can talk about what the word actually means."],[2081.7,"That's not that I'm really smart."],[2083.3,"That's just like I've memorized a lot of definitions in a Greek lexicon and most people haven't bothered right and wouldn't want to if they're just translating it into actual English."],[2101.03,"Is is a big chunk of what I sa sow that it makes sense."],[2105.55,"Yeah, and I have a Protestant friend, older guy, you know, who who's really uh been a Christian like his all his life."],[2114.55,"And uh but he teached me a lot, but I was talking to him recently and I was telling him about you know the church I was going to and um telling him about it, and uh he said he's like you know it just read your Bible and compare what the Bible says and see if what they're doing is biblical."],[2134.67,"And then it's like, well how can I do that?"],[2138.83,"Yeah. It's like you you gotta you can get so much out of if you know like what Father Stevens talking about."],[2149.23,"You know, I wouldn't never gotten anything close to that."],[2151.97,"Rather than just reading it myself."],[2153.61,"So well, and it's it's it's translated by somebody."],[2157.37,"Yeah, yeah, yeah."],[2158.81,"Or a group of somebody."],[2160.95,"Who are making compromises or who have their own ideas and tilts."],[2166.55,"And even if you spent some time studying Greek and Hebrew, you're learning Greek and Hebrew from Greek and Hebrew textbooks."],[2175.77,"And you're getting meanings from lexicons that were put together by somebody."],[2181.53,"At some point in time."],[2183.34,"So there's always these levels of."],[2186.54,"But again, like I like I said originally in response, that's normal."],[2193.27,"God didn't drop the Bible in Corinth, he sent them Saint Paul."],[2198.87,"And Saint Paul had the Hebrew scriptures with him."],[2202.47,"And then wrote them letters"],[2203.23,"And taught them and explained them to them."],[2209.24,"To do more teaching and explaining."],[2211.72,"And this, I mean, we have two of his multiple flags."],[2216.97,"And they had the letters."],[2219.69,"They had him visiting them and talking with them."],[2226.01,"Timothy was probably there at some time or another."],[2228.49,"Yeah, St. Timothy was there."],[2229.69,"Apollos was there."],[2231.13,"So you know, what we're getting of of what St."],[2236.89,"Paul taught is just a wind, just a peep, just a window into his teaching, not the whole thing."],[2246.25,"No We don't have every word he said."],[2248.15,"We don't have every word Jesus said."],[2252.31,"Um there are two different times where St."],[2254.79,"Paul quotes Jesus and they're not in the Gospels."],[2261.03,"Um so yeah."],[2264.87,"Yeah. But as you said, we have the scriptures that God wants us to have."],[2271.32,"But God also gave us a church and church mothers."],[2276.58,"And that's the context in which he gave them."],[2279.14,"He gave them to us."],[2280.58,"The situation that we're in today, with a literacy rate this high, I mean exponentially higher than any other time in the history of the world."],[2289.42,"And with print mass printing, where you can get a cheap Bible virtually free."],[2295.1,"You can get New Testament basically free."],[2297.42,"You can just go stay at a hotel and steal the Gideon Bible."],[2299.84,"I mean, you can Yeah, you can get 23 different translations on your cell phone for free."],[2304.96,"Yeah. This is this is completely abnormal in the history of the world."],[2309.94,"This is a tiny window of time."],[2313.22,"In the history of Christianity, let alone the world, forget the history of the world."],[2316.1,"This is your Christianity."],[2318.23,"There's this tiny little sliver where it's even possible for someone to pick up a Bible outside of the context of the church and her teaching."],[2326.95,"So that's not only not the norm."],[2330.17,"That's a w weird small aberration."],[2334.07,"That that that would even happen."],[2336.96,"That that would even be an option for people to do."],[2341.52,"Um I remember that the first English translations that were printed were not sold on the streets, they were put in the churches."],[2354.24,"Yes. That's where they were."],[2356.4,"Yeah. Yeah. People at that time couldn't have read them, most people anyway, and couldn't afford them if they could."],[2363.66,"Yeah. So yeah, they were in the church."],[2366.38,"And that was the place where people interfaced with them."],[2369.74,"People for almost all of Christian history primarily heard the scriptures, they didn't read them, even in church."],[2374.06,"them. They heard read out loud"],[2379.94,"Um yeah, so it's a we have a very different interface, and somehow, through various chains of events, some of our uh Protestant Christian friends have come to the idea that that's the norm, and that's how God established it and intended it to be, even though it hasn't been that way until like maybe the last 50 years."],[2404.72,"Maybe 70 years now dating myself."],[2407.44,"It's 2020. But that's that's a right, that's what three, four percent of Christian history."],[2418.24,"So let's let's. uh chapter four, verse sixteen"],[2422.92,"Therefore we do not lose heart, even though our outward man is perishing, yet the inward man is being renewed day by day."],[2431.56,"Now let's be clear."],[2433.72,"Outward man, inward man is not talking about body and soul."],[2437.8,"How do we know that?"],[2440.04,"He just talked about the resurrection and the glory of God in our mortal body a few verses ago."],[2447.26,"So when he talks about outward man, inward man."],[2451.55,"He's talking about what he's already talked about in Romans and what he talks about in other epistles, the idea that we are in Christ a new creation."],[2459.8,"That at our baptism, this new person. comes into being"],[2465.28,"And we continue to put off the old man who's still kind of lingering around."],[2469.62,"The old sinful self, keeps rearing his ugly head."],[2473.75,"And put on the new man."],[2476.25,"And form the image of Christ."],[2477.87,"So there's there's an old self that's dying and is going to die with this world."],[2482.11,"There's this new self coming into being."],[2486.24,"And St. Paul has just been talking about outward appearance."],[2490.33,"So the people who looked at St."],[2491.61,"Paul on the outside wouldn't see this radical transformation that's happened in him since Christ appeared to him."],[2500.79,"His old rabbi friends would probably just think he went nuts and joined a cult."],[2504.84,"Like they wouldn't see some transformation of him into a new person."],[2514.22,"On the outside."],[2515.92,"But that new person exists inside of him."],[2520.57,"And is going to come out and be free."],[2525.37,"And so even though all of this suffering and all this death that people see is happening, he's saying that's happening to the old me."],[2532.33,"All of that suffering, all these things I'm going through are part of helping me get rid of that old, that old person."],[2540.75,"So that the new person can more fully come to life."],[2545.47,"For our light affliction, which is but for a moment, is working for us a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory."],[2556.47,"So interestingly, he now says light affliction after all those things he went through."],[2567.11,"Why? Because he's giving perspective."],[2569.85,"This is light affliction for a time."],[2572.41,"When you put it into the perspective of eternity."],[2575.77,"When you put it into the perspective of that inward man, that new man, the age to come, the life to come, the resurrection."],[2583.29,"When you put it into that perspective, this is some low grade suffering for a little bit."],[2588.87,"We can get through this."],[2591.07,"We can get through this in the light of what's coming."],[2594.43,"Um, and that that phrase, the weight of glory, is um St."],[2600.91,"Paul using a little bit of a Semiticism."],[2603.63,"Uh the word uh for glory in Hebrew is kavod, uh, which literally means weight or heaviness."],[2613.31,"Uh it really has the idea of essence."],[2618.05,"So um, this exceeding great, this essence, the the sort of core."],[2624.14,"The of glory that's coming."],[2627.48,"So this is light."],[2629.1,"These things I'm going through now, this is light, this is little, this is, and then, but the glory that's to come is heavy."],[2636.12,"So if you picture balancing scales."],[2638.57,"Yeah, the life of the world to come way down."],[2644.42,"The sufferings now way up."],[2646.6,"Completely outweighed. Well, we do not look at the things which are seen, but at the things which are not seen."],[2656.84,"For the things which are seen are temporary, but the things which are not seen are eternal."],[2663.21,"He's saying the things you see right now: the suffering, the trouble, the strife, all the things you're going through, you see those right now."],[2671.53,"But those are all going away."],[2673.93,"They're all part of this visible world."],[2675.77,"world This, this age."],[2676.01,"They're going away"],[2680.03,"The things you don't see with your eyes, that you do see with your mind."],[2685.69,"Glory to God, those things are forever."],[2689.21,"Those things are eternal."],[2691.37,"That's where you need to keep that focus."],[2695.21,"Is on those things and not be distracted by all of these others."],[2701.77,"So that's the end of chapter four."],[2704.01,"We'll go ahead and stop here."],[2707.25,"So thank you, everybody."],[2710.13,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2717.49,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2722.85,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.96,"lv":2,"h":"2 Corinthians 4:5 - We preach Christ, not ourselves","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:5</b>: \"we do not preach ourselves, but <b>Christ Jesus the Lord</b>, and ourselves your <b>bondservants</b> for Jesus' sake\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Just talked about how he lived his life - but <b>\"this isn't about me\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">about <b>not getting in the way of Christ</b>, who he's actually preaching</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Bondservant</b> = nice way of saying <b>slave</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not only Christ's slave - <b>their</b> slave: \"In Christ, I'm your slave\"</li><li class=\"d0\">He's <b>out of the way</b>, just serving, trying to <b>connect them to Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":73.37,"lv":2,"h":"4:6 - Light shining out of darkness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:6</b>: \"the God who commanded light to shine out of darkness has shone in our hearts to give the <b>light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same God who said <b>\"Let there be light\"</b> (<b>Genesis 1</b>) - gave the command, light showed out of darkness</li><li class=\"d1\">the same God shone his light <b>into our hearts</b> so we could <b>see Christ</b>, the light of his glory</li><li class=\"d0\">Note the last part: \"in the <b>face</b> of Jesus Christ\"</li><li class=\"d1\">apostles lived with Christ; plenty in <b>Galilee and Judea</b> saw him preaching to crowds</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul had seen him personally <b>at least three times</b> (Acts)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Corinthians</b>: when had anyone there seen the <b>face of Jesus Christ</b>? not in the literal, physical sense</li></ul>"},{"t":107.54,"lv":2,"h":"The face of Christ: iconography","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Allow me to submit the <b>antiquity of iconography</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul has just been on <b>prayer, focus</b>, focusing the mind on Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Where did Corinthians <b>pray</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">not the temple daily - temple <b>sacrifices only at big feasts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no one day a week like <b>Jews</b> (or us) - no Friday/Saturday/Sunday at temple</li><li class=\"d1\">these were <b>pagan Corinthians before</b> - the vast majority</li><li class=\"d1\">practiced at their <b>hearth</b>, in the <b>family home</b></li></ul>"},{"t":197.5,"lv":3,"h":"The pagan hearth and the family gods","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">On the hearth: the <b>family gods</b> - little figures, idols, images</li><li class=\"d1\">you <b>inherited</b> your gods in the ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we have <b>pantheons</b> because we read <b>mythology</b>\" - they had <b>religious practices</b>, not mythology</li><li class=\"d1\">gods = function of <b>family</b> (ancestors served them for centuries), <b>where you were from</b>, <b>where you lived</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all got <b>piled together</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hearth = where they did the <b>cooking</b>, vital and <b>central</b> to family life</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Vesta</b> = goddess of the hearth</li><li class=\"d0\">Becoming Christian: <b>no idols</b> anymore - but <b>still going to pray</b>, still need a place</li></ul>"},{"t":337.47,"lv":3,"h":"Pompeii walls and Christian houses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pompeii</b> (Vesuvius) preserved Roman villas</li><li class=\"d1\">walls covered in imagery of <b>mythology</b>, some <b>sexually graphic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christians would have <b>covered/replaced</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">never found a house - <b>including house churches</b> - with <b>whitewashed walls</b>: they had <b>Christian iconography</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>His argument</b>: in a context of prayer, focus and connection to Christ, where would they <b>see the face of Jesus Christ</b>? → <b>iconography of Christ at the family hearth</b> where they prayed</li><li class=\"d1\">\"open to another contextual explanation\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Commentators: \"just the <b>incarnation</b>\" - true, but...</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul, writing to a Corinth in turmoil, <b>drops in a nugget on the incarnation</b> mid-discussion of prayer, focus, glory, community? \"oh yeah, by the way\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no other reading makes immediate sense to the average Corinthian</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Worth pausing on</b>: reference to <b>iconography</b>, connected to <b>prayer</b>, in the <b>50s AD</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fits how ancients understood prayer</li><li class=\"d1\">\"annoy your <b>Calvinist</b> friends\"</li></ul>"},{"t":516.99,"lv":2,"h":"4:7 - Treasure in earthen vessels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:7</b>: \"we have this <b>treasure in earthen vessels</b>, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul on the <b>glory</b> of God/Christ - making it manifest through himself</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not by being glorious</b> - by <b>getting out of the way</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lets Christ's light shine</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: the light of glory <b>contained in an earthen vessel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Bonus question, \"obscure\": which <b>OT story</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Judges - Gideon</b>, \"favorite book of sex and violence\"</li><li class=\"d1\">attacking the <b>Midianites</b>: <b>earthenware pots over torches</b> so they won't see the light as they approach</li><li class=\"d0\">Ties to his language: gospel = <b>report of a victory</b>, the Roman <b>triumph</b> procession</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>light of God's glory = torch</b>; <b>earthenware pot over it = Paul</b></li></ul>"},{"t":642.71,"lv":3,"h":"Pottery as the trash of the ancient world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Earthenware sounds nice to us - <b>craft shops, decorative pottery</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in the ancient world = <b>trash</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Broken pottery shards</b> everywhere</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>scratch paper</b> - no paper; notes scratched on shards</li><li class=\"d1\">votes in early <b>Greek democracy</b> scratched on shards</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek <b><i>ostraca</i></b> = shard → <b>ostracize</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Athens</b>: vote a troublemaker into exile on shards</li><li class=\"d1\">→ being <b>ostracized</b> = expelled, shunned</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Like <b>paper bags / plastic bags</b> from the supermarket\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: \"I'm like a <b>plastic bag</b> thrown over\" the light</li><li class=\"d0\">Why would God send \"<b>a schmuck like me</b>\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">so it's <b>obvious</b> the power isn't his</li><li class=\"d1\">people feel <b>God's power</b>, lives transformed - \"clearly not from this old paper bag\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not something Paul <b>came up with</b></li></ul>"},{"t":801.15,"lv":2,"h":"4:8-10 - Hard pressed but not crushed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:8-10</b>: \"hard pressed on every side yet <b>not crushed</b>; perplexed but <b>not in despair</b>; persecuted but <b>not forsaken</b>; struck down but <b>not destroyed</b>; always carrying about <b>the dying of the Lord Jesus</b>, that the <b>life of Jesus</b> may be manifested in our body\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Back to his <b>sufferings</b> - but also for the <b>Corinthians</b>, who've been through <b>strife</b></li><li class=\"d1\">against \"become a Christian, <b>best life now</b>\" preachers - not what they're experiencing</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>stoned, beaten with rods, run from town to town</b></li><li class=\"d1\">old limp plastic bag - but with <b>God's glory shining in his heart</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>perplexed</b> = \"vexed\" - doesn't fall into despair</li><li class=\"d0\">Carrying around the <b>dying of Jesus</b> = Christ's <b>crucifixion</b> - \"you look at him and that's what you see\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Corinthians see <b>suffering, shame, humiliation, embarrassment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">crucified <b>naked</b> - humiliation as well as torture and death</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"So that\"</b> the life of Jesus may be manifested in our body = the <b>resurrection</b></li><li class=\"d1\">suffer now → <b>participate in Christ's resurrection</b>, his <b>eternal life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ never dies again</li></ul>"},{"t":962.69,"lv":2,"h":"4:11-12 - Death at work in us, life in you","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:11-12</b>: \"we who live are always delivered to death for Jesus' sake... so death is working in us, but <b>life in you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Repeats the point</li><li class=\"d1\">life of Jesus in our <b>mortal flesh</b> → back to <b>1 Corinthians 15</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a mental thing or some afterlife - <b>resurrection and restoration of our bodies</b>, our whole selves</li><li class=\"d1\">whole selves <b>suffer now</b>, will <b>experience resurrection and healing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Theme: he is <b>their slave</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you look at me and see the <b>death</b>\" - going through it <b>for your sake</b>, to bring the light so <b>you</b> experience the resurrection</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: resurrection of the dead, or something in this present body? A: <b>yes</b> - both</li><li class=\"d1\">\"life of the world to come\" in the creed - but it <b>doesn't start there</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>eternal life starts now</b> (<b>John</b>): \"he who believes in me will never die\"</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't mean no physical death - starts now, goes on <b>into the age to come</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1079.03,"lv":3,"h":"What we do now survives into the age to come","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ministry things Paul teaches - more on this later in the epistle</li><li class=\"d1\">what we do related to <b>Jesus' ministry, God's work in this world</b> → <b>survives into the age to come</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Devil = god of this age</b>: what people worship and serve (<b>wealth, beauty</b>) leads to destruction</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>die with it</b>, end when this age ends</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's teaching, put into life now → <b>already belongs to the age to come</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ: <b>treasures in heaven vs on earth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">both result from <b>what we do here</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lay up on earth: <b>warehouse, bank account</b> → \"go <b>poof</b>\" when life/world ends</li><li class=\"d1\">laid up in heaven → <b>still there</b> in the age to come</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: most of us are a <b>mixture</b> - do the bad parts of us just go? A: Paul <b>gets to that</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how things get <b>burned up</b> - \"we will get there\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1214.28,"lv":2,"h":"4:13 - The same spirit of faith","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:13</b>: \"since we have the same <b>spirit of faith</b>, according to what is written, '<b>I believed and therefore I spoke</b>,' we also believe and therefore speak, knowing that he who raised up the Lord Jesus will <b>also raise us up</b> with Jesus and <b>present us with you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Faith</b> and <b>believe</b> = <b>same Greek word</b> - disguised in English as different concepts</li><li class=\"d1\">→ <b>\"spirit of faithfulness\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Spirit</b>: almost always <b>capitalized</b> in the NT, esp. with the <b>definite article</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he's been on the <b>Spirit vs the letter</b> all last chapter</li></ul>"},{"t":1326.7,"lv":3,"h":"Translators \"punting\": genitives","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Blank of blank</b>\" in translations = <b>punting</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>genitive</b> noun - could relate to the other noun in <b>many ways</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"of\" = they <b>decided not to figure it out</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"spirit of faithfulness\" out of context: <b>the faithful spirit</b>? the spirit <b>which causes us to be faithful</b>? any number of readings</li><li class=\"d0\">Here: quote of the <b>Psalm</b> (Orthodox Study Bible has <b>no footnote</b> on v13)</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>Spirit</b> dwells in us, lies <b>behind what is written</b></li><li class=\"d1\">translate consistently in the same sentence: \"<b>I was faithful</b> and therefore I spoke\" - \"that makes more sense\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we also are faithful and therefore speak\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The <b>speaking</b> = showing the light of God's glory</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no veil, no covering</b>, with the <b>integrity of his life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">faithful to his calling <b>even as an old bag</b>, not worthy of the message</li><li class=\"d1\">God wanted it <b>obvious it comes from him</b> - like <b>Balaam's donkey</b>: \"if a donkey's talking to you, it must be coming from God\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>faithful, obedient → he speaks</b>; urges them to do the same: <b>none of us is worthy</b>, but be faithful and shine the light <b>for the glory of God</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1540.99,"lv":3,"h":"\"Because we know\" - the resurrection","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">English <b>gerund</b> (\"<b>knowing</b>\") for a Greek <b>participle</b> = punting again - \"marked down in Greek classes in grad school\"</li><li class=\"d1\">could be \"because I know\", \"while\", \"when\"</li><li class=\"d1\">here it's <b>causal</b>: \"<b>because we know</b>\" - a committee kept the ambiguous</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Because</b> Paul knows the <b>Father who raised Jesus</b> will raise him too, and <b>present</b> him along with you</li><li class=\"d1\">why he's faithful <b>despite unworthiness and adversity</b>, why he presses on</li><li class=\"d0\">Brings it back to them: true for <b>you</b> too</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"be like me, I'm so great\" - <b>\"I'm the old pot\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">as I try to be faithful, <b>you need to be faithful</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1665.23,"lv":2,"h":"4:15 - All things for your sakes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:15</b>: \"all things are for your sakes, that <b>grace</b>, having spread through the many, may cause <b>thanksgiving</b> to abound to the glory of God\"</li><li class=\"d0\">All he's doing is for them - <b>Corinth and all the churches</b> he brings the gospel to</li><li class=\"d0\">What is he actually doing? \"Not much\" - <b>being a bag, a pot</b>, trying <b>not to get in the way</b></li><li class=\"d1\">means <b>what God is doing</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Grace = God acting</b> in his creation</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God acting through Paul</b>; likewise when they shine the light in their community, God acts through them, <b>through men</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Grace going out through his people produces <b>thanksgiving to the glory of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">from those who've received <b>that grace, healing, mercy</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1761.6,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: why verse 15 reads like platitudes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: hearing 15 cold = \"<b>platitudes, church talk</b>\"; frustrating that <b>millions read it</b> and miss the point</li><li class=\"d0\">A: problem with friends who say \"<b>just read the Bible</b> and understand it\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ethiopian eunuch</b> (Acts): Philip - \"do you understand?\" - \"<b>how can I unless someone teaches me</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no promise</b> the Bible functions by itself</li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>never dropped a Bible into a village</b> - sends people <b>with</b> it</li><li class=\"d0\">translate it into their language, teach, explain, preach</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>lead in worship</b> where Scripture is read</li><li class=\"d1\">how it functions in the Bible and through <b>all of church history</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Peter</b>: many things in Paul's epistles are <b>hard to understand</b> - \"if hard for Peter, I'm in real trouble\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1927.61,"lv":2,"h":"Christianese","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Church words</b> = an obstacle for modern people</li><li class=\"d1\">first <b>catechism</b> as a priest: \"when I said <b>prayer</b> and they said prayer, we didn't mean the same\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same with <b>God</b>; <b>grace</b>, <b>mercy</b> - no definition, certainly none in common</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christianese</b> phrases, e.g. \"<b>personal relationship with Jesus</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">what does it mean? by usual meaning: go places together, talk on the phone - obviously not literal about Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">pressed, most can't say; \"I probably said it and <b>didn't know what I meant</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Translated Scripture, prayers, hymns in Christianese → <b>whole texts with no clue</b> - jargon strung together</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Tricks that make me seem smarter\": <b>three decades studying Greek</b></li><li class=\"d1\">memorized a lot of <b>lexicon definitions</b>; not that I'm smart</li></ul>"},{"t":2105.55,"lv":3,"h":"Translation and \"just read the Bible\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Protestant friend, older: \"<b>just read your Bible</b> and see if what they're doing is biblical\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Q: how can I do that? You can get so much more from teaching than reading alone</li><li class=\"d0\">It's <b>translated by somebody</b> - a group with <b>compromises, ideas, tilts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">learning Greek/Hebrew from <b>textbooks</b>, meanings from <b>lexicons</b> put together by somebody</li><li class=\"d1\">always these <b>levels</b> - but that's normal</li></ul>"},{"t":2193.27,"lv":3,"h":"God sent Paul, not a Bible","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>God didn't drop the Bible in Corinth, he sent Saint Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul had the <b>Hebrew Scriptures</b>, taught and explained, <b>wrote letters</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Timothy</b> was there, <b>Apollos</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\">What we have = \"a <b>peep, a window</b>\" into his teaching</li><li class=\"d1\">not every word he said; not every word <b>Jesus</b> said</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>quotes Jesus twice</b> in words not in the Gospels</li><li class=\"d0\">God gave <b>Scripture</b> and also <b>a church and church mothers</b> - the context he gave them in</li></ul>"},{"t":2280.58,"lv":2,"h":"Reading the Bible alone is a modern aberration","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Today: <b>literacy exponentially higher</b> than ever, <b>mass printing</b>, free Bibles</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gideon Bible</b> from a hotel; <b>23 translations on your cell phone</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"completely <b>abnormal</b> in history\" - a <b>tiny sliver</b> even of Christian history</li><li class=\"d1\">possible to pick up a Bible <b>outside the church and her teaching</b> = \"a weird small <b>aberration</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First <b>English translations printed</b> were <b>put in the churches</b>, not sold on the streets</li><li class=\"d1\">most couldn't read, couldn't afford them</li><li class=\"d1\">church = where people <b>interfaced</b> with them</li><li class=\"d0\">For almost all Christian history people <b>heard</b> Scripture read aloud, even in church</li><li class=\"d0\">Some <b>Protestant friends</b> think the norm is how God intended - but only the last <b>~50-70 years</b>: \"three, four percent of Christian history\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2422.92,"lv":2,"h":"4:16 - Outward man and inward man","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:16</b>: \"we do not lose heart, even though our <b>outward man is perishing</b>, yet the <b>inward man is being renewed day by day</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not body and soul</b> - he just spoke of resurrection and glory in our <b>mortal body</b></li><li class=\"d0\">= <b>new creation in Christ</b> (Romans, other epistles)</li><li class=\"d1\">at <b>baptism</b> the <b>new person</b> comes into being</li><li class=\"d1\">we keep <b>putting off the old man</b> - the old sinful self \"rearing his ugly head\" - and <b>putting on the new man</b>, forming the <b>image of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">old self <b>dying with this world</b>; new self coming into being</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's <b>outward appearance</b>: old <b>rabbi friends</b> would think he \"went nuts and joined a cult\" - no visible transformation</li><li class=\"d1\">the new person is <b>inside</b>, and will come out and be <b>free</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The suffering and death people see = happening to the <b>old me</b></li><li class=\"d1\">helps me get rid of the old person → the new can <b>more fully come to life</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2545.47,"lv":3,"h":"4:17 - Light affliction, weight of glory","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:17</b>: \"our <b>light affliction</b>, which is but for a moment, is working for us a <b>far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Light\" after everything he went through - <b>perspective</b></li><li class=\"d1\">against <b>eternity</b>, the inward man, the age to come, the resurrection → \"low grade suffering for a little bit\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we can get through this in light of what's coming\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Weight of glory</b> = a <b>Semiticism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew <b><i>kavod</i></b> (glory) = <b>weight, heaviness</b> → idea of <b>essence</b>, the core</li><li class=\"d1\">now = <b>light</b>; glory to come = <b>heavy</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>balancing scales</b>: life of the world to come way down, sufferings way up - <b>completely outweighed</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2646.6,"lv":2,"h":"4:18 - Seen vs unseen","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:18</b>: \"we do not look at the things which are <b>seen</b>, but at the things which are <b>not seen</b>; the seen are <b>temporary</b>, the unseen are <b>eternal</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">What you see now - suffering, trouble, strife - all <b>part of this visible world, this age</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all <b>going away</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Things you <b>don't see with your eyes</b> but <b>see with your mind</b> - the <b>glory of God</b> - are <b>forever</b>, eternal</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Keep your focus</b> there, not distracted by the others</li><li class=\"d0\">End of chapter 4 - stop here</li></ul>"}]});
